Anti patterns - Common mistakes that lead to poor AI responses.
Here are some frequent prompt failures and how to fix them:

    Anti-pattern: The vague prompt 
        This is the classic "It doesn't work, please fix it" or "write something that does X" without  enough details.
        vague prompts force the AI to guess the context and often  result in generic advice or irrelevant code.
        The fix is straightforward: Add context and specifics.
        A good practice is to read your prompt and ask, "could this question apply to dozens of different scenarios?"
        If yes, it's too vague. Make it so specific that it could only apply to your scenario.

    Anti-pattern: The overload prompt
        This is the opposite issue: Asking the AI to do many things at once.
        For instance, "generate a complete NOde.js app with authentication, a front-end in React, and deployment scripts."
        The AI might attempt it, but youll likely get a jumbled or incomplete result, or it might ignore some parts of the request.
        The remedy is to split the tasks.
        Prioritize: do one thing at a time, as emphasized earlier.
        This makes it easier to catch mistakes and ensures the model stays focused.

    Anti-pattern: Missing the question 
        Sometimes users will present a lot of information but never clearly ask a question or specify what they need.
        For example "Here's my code."
        This can confuse the AI - it doesn't know what you want.
        Always include a clear ask, such as "Identify ay bugs in the above code", "Explain what this code doe"
        A prompt should have a purpose.
        Make sure the AI knows why you showed it some code.

    Anti-pattern: vague success criteria
        This is a subtle one- Sometimes you might ask for an optimization or improvement, but you don't define what sucess looks like.
        For example: "Make this function faster"
        Faster by what metric? if the AI doesn't know your performance constraints, it might micro-optimize something that doesn't matter or use an approach that is theoretically faster.
        We deal with this by explicitly stating goals like "reduce duplication" or "improve variable names"
        i.e "refactor this to remove global variables and use class instead."

    Anti-pattern: Ignoring Ai's clarification or output
        Sometimes the AI might respond with a clarifying queston or an assumption.
        For instance "Are you using React class components or functional components?"
        if you ignore such questions and just reiterate your request, you're missing an opportunity to improve the prompt.
        The AI is usually signaling that it needs more info or a clarification.
        Treat like a conversation - if a human misunderstood, you'd explain differently; do the same for the AI.

    Anti-pattern: Varying style or Inconsistency
        If you keep changing how you ask or mixing different formats in one go, the model can get confused.
        For example: Switching between first-person and third-person in instructions, or mixing pseudocode with actual code in a confusing way.
        Consistency helps the model parse your intent correctly.
        Also if you have a preffered style consistently mention it, otherwise the model might suggest one way in one prompt and another way later.

    Anti-pattern: Vague reference like "above code"
        If the conversation is long and you say "refactor the above code"
        The Ai might lose track and pick the wrong code snippet to refactor.
        It is safer to either quote th ecode again or specifically name the function you want refactored.
        Models have a limited attention window, and although many LLMs can refer to prior parts of the conversation, giving it explicit context again can help avoid confusion.


Finally here is a tactical way to rewriting prompts
    Identify what was missing or incorrect in the AI;s response 
    Add or emphasize that requirement in a new prompt
    Break done the request further if needed.
    Consider starting a fresh if the thread is stuck.

Being ware of these anti patterns and their solutions, you'll become much faster at adjusting your prompts on the fly.
Prompt Engineering is very much an iterative, feedback-driven process.